      "doctor_question": "If someone you trust completely—like a family member or close friend—sat down with you and showed you evidence that these patterns in your wife's messages were actually just coincidence, do you think you could be convinced, or would you still be certain the signals are real?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S019",
      "patient_response": "I don't think so, no. Even if my brother or someone sat me down with all this supposed evidence, I wouldn't buy it. I mean, they aren't the ones seeing it every day. I know my wife says the late meetings and those little changes in her work messages are just normal office stuff, but for the past year they’ve lined up way too precisely to feel accidental. It really feels like there’s a private signal in them meant to tell me something she won’t say directly. \n\nMy brother actually tried to do something like that once, years ago. Not about this, obviously, but about this used car I was buying. He brought all these Consumer Reports printouts to Thanksgiving dinner, right next to the turkey. My mother had made this incredible cranberry sauce from scratch that year—she usually just buys the canned stuff, you know, the kind that comes out still shaped like the can with the little ridges? I actually prefer the canned kind. It's nostalgic. We used to eat it every Thanksgiving at my grandmother's house in Ohio. Her house always smelled like mothballs and peppermint. She had this massive collection of porcelain dogs in her living room, hundreds of them, all staring at you while you ate... \n\nWait, sorry. What were we talking about? Oh, right, being convinced. No, I just know what I'm seeing. The signals are there, no matter what anyone else tries to show me.",
