      "patient_response": "I mean, no, there isn't really a part of me that doubts it. It's impossible to doubt it when you're living it. I know my wife keeps saying that the late meetings and those little changes in her work messages are just normal office stuff, but I've been watching this for a year now. They line up way too precisely to just be an accident. It's a private signal, she's trying to tell me something she just won't say to my face. \n\nWhich is frustrating, because communication is supposed to be the foundation of a marriage, right? That's what they say in all those couples counseling books. My sister actually bought me one of those books for Christmas a few years ago. It had this awful pastel cover with two people holding hands on a beach. I don't even like the beach. The sand gets absolutely everywhere. We went to Myrtle Beach for our honeymoon, and I swear I was still finding sand in my suitcase three years later. We stayed in this hotel that had a lazy river, but the water was always freezing cold. I ended up spending most of the trip just reading paperbacks on the balcony. I read this one fantastic mystery novel about a detective in 1920s London... \n\nBut anyway, what was the question? Oh, doubting it. No, I don't doubt it at all. I know what I'm seeing.",
